<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fortress of Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gibraltar of the North</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fortress of Luxembourg was the fortified city of Luxembourg, strengthened over centuries by Burgundian, Spanish, French, Austrian and Prussian rulers until it was called the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> of the North'. After 1815 it was a federal fortress of the German Confederation held by a Prussian garrison. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867">Treaty of London</a> of 1867 ordered its demolition; the remaining walls and the Bock casemates are now part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fortress-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="Fortress of Luxembourg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown prior to 1867 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxembourg_fortress_before_demolition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
